 "notes": [
  "Brier = mean squared error of the probability that the disruption is still not resumed at the horizon; ci95 entries are 95% paired bootstrap intervals (2000 resamples of whole test weeks) of model minus baseline, so a negative interval means the model is better.",
  "Model selected on 2025 (fitted on 2024): aft_lognormal, ridge 1; it was then refitted on 2024-03..2025-12 and evaluated once on 2026. The other family is reported as other_family_* (its best ridge on 2025).",
  "Labels are administrative: \"Service resumed\" (or VIA ENDED) in the liveticker, not the first train through; the clock starts at the first published Interrupted/Limited version, not at the physical event.",
  "Incidents without an end (withdrawn or never closed: 30) are excluded, not censored.",
  "operator = the first announced end turned into a probability by a logistic fit on the training years (ln announced/horizon, no-end flag, at-least flag); operator_point = 1 if the announced end is after the horizon (historical rate when none); cause = per EG code survival shrunk to the prior (2 pseudo-incidents); prior = Kaplan-Meier of the training durations.",
  "The conditional rows compare S(t)/S(e) (start features only, as in the contract) with the latest announced end known at e (calibrated on training incidents still open at e).",
  "Small sample: about 140 test incidents; differences of 0.01 Brier are within noise unless the interval says otherwise."
 ],
